{
 "lead": "Double-sideband suppressed-carrier modulation (DSB-SC) sends the two sidebands of AM and leaves out the carrier. Since the carrier carries no message, every watt now goes into the sidebands. The price is at the receiver: the envelope of DSB-SC is not the message, so the receiver must make its own copy of the carrier, at exactly the right frequency and phase, multiply and low-pass filter. This is coherent (synchronous) detection. DSB-SC carries the stereo difference signal in FM broadcasting and the colour signals of analog television, and it is the parent of SSB and QAM.",
 "prereq": "You need: the AM spectrum and power from <a href=\"#am-modulation-index-spectrum-and-power\">AM: modulation index, spectrum and power</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The DSB-SC wave and its spectrum",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A product modulator multiplies the message m(t), in volts, by the carrier cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t. Its output is"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "s(t) = A<sub>c</sub> m(t) cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t",
     "where": "A<sub>c</sub> is the multiplier's gain, a pure number, so s(t) is in volts like m(t); f<sub>c</sub> is the carrier frequency in hertz."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>S(f) = (A<sub>c</sub>/2)[M(f − f<sub>c</sub>) + M(f + f<sub>c</sub>)]</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "S(f) and M(f) are the Fourier transforms of s(t) and m(t). The message spectrum is copied to ±f<sub>c</sub> at half height."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "There is no impulse at ±f<sub>c</sub>. The reason is that the message has zero average value, so M(f) has no impulse at f = 0 to be shifted up. A message band from 0 to W hertz becomes a band from f<sub>c</sub> − W to f<sub>c</sub> + W, so the bandwidth is 2W, the same as AM. For a tone m(t) = A<sub>m</sub> cos 2πf<sub>m</sub>t the wave has just two lines, each of amplitude A<sub>c</sub>A<sub>m</sub>/2, at f<sub>c</sub> − f<sub>m</sub> and f<sub>c</sub> + f<sub>m</sub>."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In time, s(t) is a carrier whose amplitude follows m(t) and goes negative with it. At every zero of the message the carrier phase jumps by 180°, as the figure shows. The envelope is |A<sub>c</sub> m(t)|, a rectified copy of the message, so an envelope detector gives a distorted output."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Coherent detection and the effect of phase and frequency errors",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The receiver multiplies the incoming wave by a local carrier cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t + φ), where φ is the phase error in radians, and passes the product through a low-pass filter (LPF) of cutoff W. Using cos A cos B = ½[cos(A − B) + cos(A + B)]:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>s(t) cos(2πf<sub>c</sub>t + φ) = (A<sub>c</sub>/2) m(t) cos φ + (A<sub>c</sub>/2) m(t) cos(4πf<sub>c</sub>t + φ)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "y(t) = (A<sub>c</sub>/2) m(t) cos φ",
     "where": "y(t) is the LPF output in volts: the term around 2f<sub>c</sub> is removed, the baseband term stays."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "φ = 0: full output (A<sub>c</sub>/2) m(t).",
      "φ = 60°: half output, since cos 60° = 0.5.",
      "φ = 90°: zero output. This is the quadrature null effect.",
      "φ drifting slowly: the output fades up and down as cos φ changes."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A frequency error Δf makes the local carrier cos 2π(f<sub>c</sub> + Δf)t, and the output becomes (A<sub>c</sub>/2) m(t) cos 2πΔft: the message multiplied by a slow beat. A 1 kHz tone with Δf = 5 Hz comes out as two tones at 995 Hz and 1005 Hz, heard as a tone that swells and fades five times a second. Both errors mean the local carrier must be locked to the incoming one."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The quadrature null effect has a use. Two messages can share one carrier: m<sub>1</sub>(t) cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t + m<sub>2</sub>(t) sin 2πf<sub>c</sub>t. A receiver multiplying by cos gets m<sub>1</sub> with zero m<sub>2</sub>, and by sin gets m<sub>2</sub>. Some syllabi call this quadrature-carrier multiplexing (QCM) or quadrature amplitude modulation; it doubles the use of a 2W band."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Getting the carrier back: pilot, squaring loop and Costas loop",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Because no carrier is sent, the receiver must recover it. Three methods are common:"
    },
    {
     "type": "bullets",
     "items": [
      "<b>Pilot carrier.</b> The transmitter sends a small, low-power carrier. The receiver filters it out and uses it, after amplification, as the local carrier. FM stereo sends a 19 kHz pilot and doubles it to 38 kHz.",
      "<b>Squaring loop.</b> Squaring s(t) gives A<sub>c</sub>²m²(t)(1 + cos 4πf<sub>c</sub>t)/2. Since m²(t) has a nonzero average, there is a line at 2f<sub>c</sub>. A narrow filter or phase-locked loop (PLL) picks it out and a divide-by-2 gives f<sub>c</sub>, with a 180° ambiguity.",
      "<b>Costas loop.</b> Two multipliers use a voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO) output, one in phase (I branch) and one shifted by 90° (Q branch). After low-pass filtering the I arm gives (A<sub>c</sub>/2)m(t) cos φ and the Q arm (A<sub>c</sub>/2)m(t) sin φ. Their product, (A<sub>c</sub>²/8)m²(t) sin 2φ, is a control voltage that is zero only when φ = 0 and drives the VCO into lock. The I arm is then the demodulated output."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "DSB-SC against AM: for the same sidebands DSB-SC needs only the sideband power, but its receiver needs a locked oscillator. The bandwidth is 2W in both."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A 2 V peak, 1 kHz tone drives a product modulator of gain A<sub>c</sub> = 10 with a 100 kHz carrier. The output feeds 50 Ω. Write the DSB-SC wave and its sidebands, find the transmitted power, and compare it with AM that has the same sidebands at m = 1. Find the coherent detector output for φ = 0 and φ = 30°.",
   "steps": [
    "s(t) = 10 × 2 cos 2π1000t × cos 2π100000t = 20 cos 2π1000t cos 2π100000t V.",
    "20 cos A cos B = 10 cos(A − B) + 10 cos(A + B): LSB 10 cos 2π99000t V and USB 10 cos 2π101000t V, that is 10 V lines at 99 kHz and 101 kHz; bandwidth 2 kHz.",
    "Power: each sideband 10²/(2 × 50) = 1 W, total 2 W. No carrier term.",
    "AM with the same 10 V sidebands at m = 1 needs mA<sub>c</sub>/2 = 10 V, so a 20 V carrier with P<sub>c</sub> = 20²/(2 × 50) = 4 W and P<sub>t</sub> = 4 × 1.5 = 6 W.",
    "Saving: (6 − 2)/6 = 66.7 % of the AM power.",
    "Detector, φ = 0: y(t) = (A<sub>c</sub>/2) m(t) = 5 × 2 cos 2π1000t = 10 cos 2π1000t V.",
    "φ = 30°: y(t) = 10 cos 30° cos 2π1000t = 8.66 cos 2π1000t V, a 13.4 % loss in amplitude."
   ],
   "answer": "Sidebands of 10 V at 99 and 101 kHz; 2 W against 6 W for AM (66.7 % saved); output 10 cos 2π1000t V at φ = 0 and 8.66 cos 2π1000t V at φ = 30°."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "DSB-SC is not AM with a small carrier. The carrier is zero, the envelope is |A<sub>c</sub> m(t)| and an envelope detector distorts.",
  "A 90° phase error gives zero output, not half. Half output needs 60°, since cos 60° = 0.5.",
  "A frequency error is not a fixed loss: the output is m(t) multiplied by cos 2πΔft, a beat.",
  "Do not draw a line at f<sub>c</sub>. A tone gives only f<sub>c</sub> − f<sub>m</sub> and f<sub>c</sub> + f<sub>m</sub>; the bandwidth is still 2W.",
  "The squaring loop output is at 2f<sub>c</sub>, so it must be divided by 2, and the recovered phase can be 180° off."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Write the DSB-SC wave for a message m(t) and give its bandwidth.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "s(t) = A<sub>c</sub> m(t) cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t; bandwidth 2W for a message of bandwidth W."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does the DSB-SC spectrum have no line at f<sub>c</sub>?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "The message has zero average, so M(f) has no impulse at f = 0. Shifting M(f) to ±f<sub>c</sub> therefore puts no impulse there."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A 4 V, 2 kHz tone drives a product modulator of gain 20 with a 500 kHz carrier. Find the sideband frequencies and amplitudes.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Each sideband is A<sub>c</sub>A<sub>m</sub>/2 = 20 × 4/2 = 40 V, at 498 kHz and 502 kHz."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "The detector of the worked example has a 60° phase error. Find the output.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "y(t) = 10 cos 60° cos 2π1000t = 5 cos 2π1000t V, half the zero-error output."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "What is the quadrature null effect, and how does QCM use it?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "A 90° phase error makes cos φ = 0 and the output zero. QCM sends m<sub>1</sub> on cos and m<sub>2</sub> on sin of the same carrier; each coherent detector sees its own message and nulls the other."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "In a Costas loop the I arm gives (A<sub>c</sub>/2)m(t) cos φ and the Q arm (A<sub>c</sub>/2)m(t) sin φ. Show why the loop locks at φ = 0.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "The product of the arms is (A<sub>c</sub>²/8)m²(t) sin 2φ. m²(t) is never negative, so the sign of the control voltage is the sign of sin 2φ: it pushes the VCO towards φ = 0 and is zero there, where the I arm holds the full message."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Compare DSB-SC and AM for one tone at m = 1 in power and receiver cost.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "DSB-SC sends only the sidebands, one third of the AM power at m = 1 (2 W against 6 W in the example). AM can use a diode envelope detector; DSB-SC needs carrier recovery and a coherent detector."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Binary phase-shift keying is DSB-SC with a ±1 message, so the Costas loop returns in digital receivers. QAM, used in Wi-Fi and cable modems, is quadrature-carrier multiplexing with multi-level digital messages. Search next for Costas loop, carrier recovery and QAM.",
